# Engineering Standards

## Clean Code

- Use domain language: `Ticket`, `Status`, `Priority`, `Comment`, `Role`
- Keep functions small and focused; modules cohesive
- No duplicated logic — status transitions have one source: `statusTransition.ts`
- No magic strings or numbers — use constants and enums for status, priority, role, error codes
- Comment only non-obvious business logic; code must be self-documenting
- Eliminate dead code; prefer readability over cleverness

## SOLID (apply pragmatically — do not over-engineer)

| Principle | This project |
| --------- | ------------ |
| Single Responsibility | Controllers orchestrate; services enforce rules; validators validate shape |
| Open/Closed | Extend via new services/endpoints; never modify state machine ad hoc per route |
| Liskov Substitution | Service interfaces behave consistently; errors thrown predictably |
| Interface Segregation | Small focused modules — avoid god services |
| Dependency Inversion | Services depend on Prisma abstraction; routes depend on services |

## Design Principles

- **Separation of Concerns** — routes / controllers / services / data access layers
- **DRY** — one state machine, one error format, one API client layer
- **KISS** — simplest solution that satisfies requirements
- **YAGNI** — no auth, pagination, soft delete, or optimistic locking unless explicitly in scope
- **High cohesion** — ticket logic in ticket module; comment logic in comment module
- **Low coupling** — frontend consumes API contracts; no shared DB access from client
- **Composition over inheritance** — React functional components + hooks
